A||N||4||kornholio||clicking sound from the front||||5||
Z||000000||kornholio||10-03-2000||09:18 PM||||Just today there has been a clicking sound coming from the left front wheel area.  I have no idea what is causing it.  The clicking gets faster when I speed up and slower when I slow down.  When I am driving slowly, you can't hear it.  Could this be a sign that the wheel bearings need to be repacked?  Or is there just something rubbing against the wheel?  Any suggestions on how I could diagnose this problem?  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-03-2000||11:19 PM||||Sounds like something is rubbing i.e. brake calipers.  Jack the front of the car up and rotate the wheel where the sound comes from.  Pull the wheel off to check for wear marks on the wheel.  Or it may be bearing, but I always got a continuous grinding noise when bearings went.  Scored the race.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SibERThuG||10-03-2000||11:26 PM||||I have the same problem on the same side. It is the wheel bearings, which can also get sloppy and cause terrible handling and cornering. It can also cause wear and play in the control arm bushings. The bearing probably needs to be replaced completly, which goes for $250.00 without labor. I don't think it can be re-packed, or so I was told. I could have been misinformed. If I was, please correct me. I would rather repack it for $50, rather than pay $250. <P>------------------<BR>Robbie<BR>88' Formula||||reg||4
Z||000003||kornholio||10-04-2000||12:40 PM||||I found the problem.  It was a rock the size of a quarter stuck on the inside of the wheel!!!||||reg||14
Z||000004||theogre||10-04-2000||08:00 PM||||Nice the noise was something simple for a change.<P>Siber you must have an 88..... all the others front wheel bearings are standard conical bearings and cost less than $50 for all 4 bearings and 2 seals. Takes about 1-2 hours to replace both sides.<BR>||||reg||1
